da91d","fbc297305858e632","fc0478d6da04568a","fc7abce528c734da","fcc9478d20e2e3b3","fd227555b428015b","fe153b61ab4217cd","fe1d8be92f7734fa","ff26b460ea1bd80a"],"groups":["4211096551f1501e","20ae9ea45f249323","0dd8675d3961d11f","9dc9a648d8df5d4e","d00669b4e1f8b38a","7d42e632357fa655","3c992dc990aa1a05","4a84f438cce15599"],"findings":"**Mismatches**\n\n1. **Lint reports \"clean\" when the rating checks can't run (group `lint-command`, `rating-issues`). Verified.** `tryAnalyze` returns null on a malformed `.gloss/config.json`, and `run()` then skips every check that needs the analysis. I ran this in `/tmp/git-gud-obbfGo/scratch/cfgprobe`. With a valid config, `lint HEAD~2` on a gloss with an unrated group printed `no-impact`, `1 finding`. After I committed a broken config on main, the same gloss printed `warning: signals unavailable: …` on stderr, then `clean`, exit 0. The `--json` output was `findings: []`. The README and the skill say findings are advisory and that \"clean\" means nothing is wrong, so a reader can take this as a pass. The stderr warning is the only signal, and it is easy to miss. Either lint should fail or mark its output as incomplete when analysis is unavailable, or the README should say that \"clean\" is conditional on the analysis running.\n\n2. **Hunk h8 is filed under the wrong group (`rating-issues`).** The `unknownServes` export and the import change in `validate.ts` serve only the `unknown-serves` check in lint. They have nothing to do with ratings. The intent line (\"rating and serves checks\") covers this loosely, but the hunk belongs with `lint-command`, or the intent should be narrowed.\n\n3. **Garbled comment (`lint-command`, read).** `lint.ts` says \"Text from a note is findingsed here, once, for both outputs.\" It looks like a bad search-and-replace. It should say \"sanitized\" or \"escaped\".\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n4. **`rating-issues` is labelled `required` with `serves: task`. It is `supporting`, serving `lint-command`.** Nothing in the task needs add's messages turned into data. The reason for the change is that lint should not duplicate the rules. That is a design choice, and the author's own alternative (\"reimplement inside lint\") is a plausible one. The `add` output text is unchanged, and `impactwarn.test.ts` passes. The label overstates it, and its `if removed` line is a design argument, not a requirement. Calling it `supporting` makes the dependency on lint explicit.\n\n5. **`lint-command`, `lint-register`, `lint-docs` as `required` / `supporting`.** These are accurate. The lint command is the task. The docs are needed for agents to find it.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n6. None are strong. The `CONFIG_EDIT_NOTE` path is not reported by lint at all (`configEdit` is computed and then dropped), which is consistent with the nine-code list. It is not a removal target, but it is a silent gap, see missing risks.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n7. `missing-risks` uses `a.attention?.level ?? an?.derived.level`, so a gloss with no attention and a failed analysis gets no level check. Falling back to a level-independent check would avoid a second silent path. This is minor.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n8. **Silent skip on failed analysis** (see finding 1). The author did not list this. It also covers `test-serves-nothing`, which depends on `testsOnly` from the analysis, and `mechanical-on-floor`.\n\n9. **`configEdit` is dropped** (read). `ratingIssues` returns it, but lint never prints it. A reader who relies on lint will not learn that the config was edited in the diff. This is a judgment call, but it should be stated.\n\n10. **Validation error order changed** (read). In `validate.ts`, unknown-serves errors are now pushed before the self-serve check, not interleaved. The tests pass, but any consumer that compares the error list by position would see a change.\n\nChecks I ran: `lint`, `impactwarn` and `gloss` test files (106 pass, 0 fail) and `tsc --noEmit` (clean). The author's `mechanical-on-floor` risk note is accurate: lockfile, test, docs and generated hunks are `inert` in `signals.ts`, so mechanical groups made only of them are skipped. A manifest is not inert, so it is still reported, as the README says.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
